Start();
include(unitTests);
search reset stack Grokking;
search UnitTests list;

/*****************************************************************************
    manyScratch -- the fixture for the KANT `emitMany` (incant/genMany).

    WHY IT DOES NOT GO THROUGH genParse. The C++ fork in emitMany does not
    exist yet (it is foreman's, and round 2's report carries the exact diff),
    so `genParse('ScafC')` still runs the C++ body. This fixture therefore
    drives `spellMany` DIRECTLY, from a hand-built MANY plan node whose two
    attributes are shaped exactly as planRule builds them
    (genParse.rtn:469-471 `min`, genParse.rtn:516-518 `site`).

    ⚠ SO IT PROVES THE EMISSION AND NOT THE SEAM, and that distinction is the
    honest half of this round. `genLadder/rung5.target` is still produced by
    the C++ emitMany today. When the fork lands, rung5 becomes the seam's own
    oracle and this fixture keeps covering the two REFUSAL arms, which no rung
    reaches.

    THE ORACLE: stderr lines 1-10 must equal `genLadder/rung5.target` lines
    1-10 -- the `manyScafC1` helper -- BYTE FOR BYTE. The helper is emitted
    FIRST, before anything else reaches stderr, so the extraction is a plain
    head and cannot drift.

        $B incant/manyScratch > out 2> err;  echo "EXIT=$?"
        sed -n '1,10p' genLadder/rung5.target > want
        sed -n '1,10p' err > got
        diff want got

    ⚠ TAKE `$?` DIRECTLY FROM THE BINARY, NEVER THROUGH A PIPE -- this project's
    shell is zsh and `${PIPESTATUS[0]}` expands to NOTHING there, so every run
    reads as passing (corpus CLAIM KANT-24/PIPESTATUS).

    THE ROWS:
        full   site + min      the rung-5 helper, byte-exact, answer "1"
        bare   neither         refusal, answer "0"
        half   site only       refusal, answer "0" -- the arm a single
                               combined guard would have hidden

    EACH CASE GETS ITS OWN ACTION so that each has its OWN `site`/`min`
    locals. `+%` attaches the local NODE itself, and the attribute lookup is by
    TAG, so the locals have to be named `site` and `min`; three actions is how
    three independent ones are had.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT, checked first and by name. An incant parse failure
    abandons the rest of the file and STILL EXITS 0, so a truncated run is
    otherwise indistinguishable from a short successful one.

    RUN:   <binary> incant/manyScratch
*****************************************************************************/
registry(fILEs);
define genMany File='incant/genMany'; ;
include(genMany);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Maniers UnitTests list;

register(ManyScratch);
define
    caseFull code={
        site = "ScafC1";
        min  = "1";
        many = new("MANY");
        many +% site;
        many +% min;
        return spellMany(many);
        };
    caseBare code={
        many = new("MANY");
        return spellMany(many);
        };
    caseHalf code={
        site = "ScafC1";
        many = new("MANY");
        many +% site;
        return spellMany(many);
        };
    /*  ⚠ `say` IS A SEPARATE ACTION AND THAT IS NOT STYLE. `$` toggles a
        GLOBAL (useDefaultSpace) that is reset to true at ACTION ENTRY and NOT
        on return, so after runAll has called spellMany the flag is whatever
        spellMany left -- and a `$` in runAll would then turn spacing ON,
        printing `full= 1` instead of `full=1`. Entering a fresh action resets
        it, so the assertion's own spelling stops depending on what it just
        called. Presence-with-value, per standing rule H4: the answer is
        printed unconditionally and its VALUE compared, never its absence.  */
    say argument code={
        cerr $"MANY ANSWER=" argument:;
        };
    runAll code={
        full = caseFull();
        bare = caseBare();
        half = caseHalf();
        say(full);
        say(bare);
        say(half);
        };
    ;

runAll();
print "MS SENTINEL":;
stop();
